  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PGIII Biraturaba Twese 2_SADC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme builds on the foundations and achievements of the Programme Grant II (PGII) (and preceding IA funded programmes), aimed to deepen impact by developing and sustaining the agency of primary stakeholders and local partners to self-direct change. Building on achievements and based on expertise, we will continue to work in the thematic areas of governance and human rights, gender equality and social and economic inclusion, and peacebuilding and conflict prevention, and will identify entry points for an increased focus on climate action. We will take a holistic approach to addressing the root causes of&#xa0;inequalities at the community and country level, in line with triple nexus principles.
In Burundi regressive cultural and religious norms and practices favour the dominance of men over women and perpetuate acceptance of high levels of physical and economic exploitation of women and other legally recognised social gtoups such as Batwa, returnees, disable people, and albinos. Rapid population growth linked to weak sexual and reproductive health knowledge and rights further contributes to unequal power dynamics and limits the ability of women and girls to participate fully in the public sphere. Some progress has been made with the introduction of policies (such as the National Gender Policy 2021-2025), but implementation is slow, budget is inadequate, and mechanisms for policy implementation, when they exist, are weak.
SADC supports high-level advocacy for conducive legal framework and policies to serve in the socially responsible sexual and reproductive health and supports ongoing efforts to strengthen peacebuilding policies and structures at all levels: household, community, and country-level.</narrative>
  </description>
